# Preparations

Load the necessary libraries

```{r}
#| label: libraries
#| output: false
#| eval: true
#| warning: false
#| message: false
#| cache: false

library(tidyverse)  #for data wrangling etc
library(cmdstanr)   #for cmdstan
library(brms)       #for fitting models in STAN
library(standist)   #for exploring distributions
library(coda)       #for diagnostics
library(bayesplot)  #for diagnostics
library(DHARMa)     #for residual diagnostics
library(rstan)      #for interfacing with STAN
library(emmeans)    #for marginal means etc
library(broom)      #for tidying outputs
library(tidybayes)  #for more tidying outputs
library(HDInterval) #for HPD intervals
library(ggeffects)  #for partial plots
library(broom.mixed)#for summarising models
library(posterior)  #for posterior draws
library(ggeffects)  #for partial effects plots
library(patchwork)  #for multi-panel figures
library(bayestestR) #for ROPE
library(easystats)     #framework for stats, modelling and visualisation
library(mgcv)
library(gratia)
theme_set(theme_grey()) #put the default ggplot theme back
source('helperFunctions.R')
```


# Scenario

The Australian Institute of Marine Science (AIMS) have a long-term
inshore marine water quality monitoring program in which water samples
are collected and analysed from sites (reef.alias) across the GBR numerous times
per year.  The focus of this program is to report long-term condition and change
in water quality parameters.

Although we do have latitude and longitudes, the nature of the spatial design
predominantly reflects a series of transects that start near the mouth of a
major river and extend northwards, yet mainly within the open coastal zone.  As
a result, this design is not well suited to any specific spatial analyses (since
they are mainly one dimensional).

![AIMS water quality monitoring](../resources/AIMS_wq.jpg){#fig-aims width="600" height="325"}


LATITUDE LONGITUDE reef.alias Water_Samples Region Subregion Season waterYear NOx
-------- --------- ---------- ------------- ------ --------- ------ --------- ---
-16.1    145.      Cape Trib… AIMS          Wet T… Barron D… Dry    2008      0.830
-16.1    145.      Cape Trib… AIMS          Wet T… Barron D… Wet    2008      0.100
-16.1    145.      Cape Trib… AIMS          Wet T… Barron D… Dry    2009      0.282
-16.1    145.      Cape Trib… AIMS          Wet T… Barron D… Wet    2009      1.27
-16.1    145.      Cape Trib… AIMS          Wet T… Barron D… Dry    2009      0.793
-16.1    145.      Cape Trib… AIMS          Wet T… Barron D… Dry    2010      0.380
\...     \...      \...       \...          \...   \...      \...   \...      \...

: Format of aims.wq.csv data file {#tbl-aims .table-condensed}

--------------     ---------------------------------------------------------------------
**LATITUDE**       - Latitudinal coordinate
**LONGITUDE**      - Longitudinal coordinate
**reef.alias**     - Internal AIMS reef name
**Water_Samples**  - Categorical label of who collected the data
**Region**         - The MMP region
**Subregion**      - The MMP subregion
**Season**         - A categorical listing of Wet or Dry
**waterYear**      - The water year (1st Oct - 30 Sept) to which the data are attached
**Date**           - The date the sample was collected
**Mnth**           - The month the sample was collected
**NOx**            - Nitrite and Nitrate
--------------     ---------------------------------------------------------------------

: Description of the variables in the aims data file {#tbl-aims1 .table-condensed}

# Read in the data

```{r}
#| label: readData
#| cache: false
wq <- read_csv("../data/aims_wq.csv", trim_ws = TRUE)
head(wq)

#NOx is never negative or 0, so continuous data and likely a gamma distribution
#tweedy distribution allows zeros within gamma (mixture of poisson and gamma)
```
# Data preparations
Region (fixed) - cat
Subregion (fixed) - cat
Site (random) - cat
```{r}
#| label: data prep
#| eval: true
#| echo: true

wq <- wq |> 
  mutate(Site = factor(Site),
         Region = factor(Region),
         Subregion = factor(Subregion),
         Season = factor(Season))
```

# Exploratory data analysis

```{r}
#| label: visualize data
#| eval: true
#| echo: true

wq |> 
  ggplot(aes(y = NOx, x = Date)) +
  geom_point()

#hard to see trends...could make y-axis log scaled
wq |> 
  ggplot(aes(y = NOx, x = Date)) +
  geom_point() +
  scale_y_log10()
#there are values very close to 0, possibly the detection limit of the device

#facet
wq |> 
  ggplot(aes(y = NOx, x = Date)) +
  geom_point() +
  scale_y_log10() +
  facet_wrap(~Region + Site, scales = "free_y") +
  geom_smooth()
```

Model formula:
$$
\begin{align}
y_i &\sim{} \mathcal{N}(\mu_i, \sigma^2)\\
\mu_i &=\beta_0 + f(Date_i) + f(Month_i)
\end{align}
$$

where $\beta_0$ is the y-intercept. $f(Date)$ and $f(Month)$ indicate the additive smoothing functions of the long-term temporal trends and the annual seasonal trends respectively.

```{r}
#| label: add date and month
#| eval: true
#| echo: true

wq <- wq |> 
  mutate(dt_num = lubridate::decimal_date(Date)) |> 
  mutate(Mnth = month(Date)) |> 
  mutate(NOx_flag = ifelse(NOx == 0.01, "left", "none")) #censoring data thats on the limit of detection

```

# Simple model (Double Island)

```{r}
#| label: subset data
#| eval: true
#| echo: true

#subset to Double Island
wq_sub <- wq |> 
  filter(Site == "Double Island") |> 
  droplevels()

```


## Exploratory data analysis
```{r}
#| label: visualize
#| eval: true
#| echo: true

wq_sub |> 
  ggplot(aes(y = NOx, x = Date)) +
  geom_point() +
  scale_y_log10() +
  geom_smooth()
```

## Fit the model
:::: {.panel-tabset}
```{r}
#| label: gamma formula
#| eval: true
#| echo: true

#cens = censoring by NOx_flag
#s = smooth by date numeric (dt_num)
wq_form <- bf(NOx | cens(NOx_flag) ~
                s(dt_num), family = Gamma(link = "log"))

```
### Assessing default priors
```{r}
#| label: default priors
#| eval: true
#| echo: true

get_prior(wq_form, data = wq_sub)

wq_sub |> 
  summarise(Median = median(log(NOx)), MAD = mad(log(NOx)))

priors <- prior(normal(-3.1, 1), class = "Intercept") +
  prior(normal(0, 2), class = "b") +
  prior(student_t(3, 0, 10), class = 'sds') +
  prior(gamma(0.01, 0.01), class = 'shape')
```

```{r}
#| label: fit model
#| eval: true
#| echo: true

wq_sub_brm1 <- brm(wq_form,
                  data = wq_sub,
                  prior = priors,
                  sample_prior = 'only',
                  iter = 3000,
                  warmup = 1000,
                  chains = 3, cores = 3,
                  thin = 5,
                  refresh = 100,
                  control = list(adapt_delta = 0.99, max_treedepth = 20),
                  backend = "cmdstanr",
                  seed = 123
                  )

```

#### conditional_effects
```{r}
#| label: conditional plot
#| eval: true
#| echo: true

wq_sub_brm1 |> 
  conditional_effects() |> 
  plot(points = TRUE) |> 
  _[[1]] +
  scale_y_log10() +
  geom_point(data = wq_sub, inherit.aes = FALSE,
             aes(y = NOx, x = dt_num))

#priors are a bit wide
```

```{r}
#| label: run model with data
#| eval: true
#| echo: true

wq_sub_brm2 <- update(wq_sub_brm1, 
                      sample_prior = "yes", 
                      cores = 3, 
                      seed =123, 
                      refresh = 0)
```

```{r}
#| label: conditional effects plot again
#| eval: true
#| echo: true

wq_sub_brm2 |> 
  conditional_effects() |> 
  plot(points = TRUE) |> 
  _[[1]] +
  scale_y_log10() +
  geom_point(data = wq_sub, inherit.aes = FALSE,
             aes(y = NOx, x = dt_num))
```


The following link provides some guidance about defining priors.
[https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/wiki/Prior-Choice-Recommendations]


